# ============================================================
# ~/.claude .gitignore — FAIL-CLOSED (default-deny).
# Ignore everything; explicitly opt in ONLY versionable machinery.
# Rationale: this dir mixes secrets (.credentials.json), confidential
# deal terms (hooks/confidential-terms.json) and transient runtime
# state with the config worth tracking. Default-deny prevents an
# accidental secret commit — matches the firm's fail-closed doctrine.
# To track a new machinery dir, add an explicit "!/<dir>/" line.
# ============================================================

# 1) Ignore everything at root by default (matches dotfiles too)
/*

# 2) Opt IN the machinery worth versioning
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/README.md
!/CLAUDE.md
!/settings.json
!/keybindings.json
!/confidential-repos.json
!/uuid-baseline.json
!/check-uuid-drift.sh
!/daily-setup-audit-prompt.md
!/scheduled-tasks-starter-pack.md
!/hooks/
!/commands/
!/agents/
!/skills/
!/maintenance/
# Reviewable settings.json proposals awaiting an explicit apply (hooks-audit
# 2026-09-30). Tracked so a proposal survives its worktree and ships with the
# branch; archived once applied (the hooks-audit one was applied 2026-10-18 and
# lives at _archive/2026-10-18_settings-proposed-fold/APPLY.md). Kept for future
# proposals. Tests never read it as a stand-in for settings.json
# (hooks/tests/lib/settings_proposal.py).
!/_proposed/
!/docs/
!/ora-ops-plugin/
!/.github/
# (2026-07-16) Named Workflow scripts + their test rigs — versioned for durability, same
# rationale as 2b/2c: cc-audit-army.js was unversioned when parameterized; its pre-change
# prompt snapshot (_tests/cc-mode-prompts.snapshot.json) byte-pins the proven cc pipeline
# and must survive scratchpad cleanup. Contents pre-checked: no secrets, no deal terms.
!/workflows/
# (2026-07-16) Four opt-ins ratified by Gabriel after the probe's new backup-coverage
# sweep (check 12) flagged them. Each scrubbed pre-commit: zero confidential-term hits
# (E. Fulton mentions are declassified historical references, matching already-tracked
# memory files); the ONE embedded secret found (FRED API key in a retired
# ora-debt-maturity-snapshot SKILL.md) was redacted to {{FRED_API_KEY}} before opt-in.
!/prompts/
!/mcp-servers/
!/obsidian-fleet-mirror/
# live runtime state rewritten every few minutes by write-fleet.ps1 -- tracking it
# would recreate the doctrine-status.json perpetual-dirty-tree noise; scripts only.
obsidian-fleet-mirror/session-state.json
# 2026-07-25: fleet-session-hook.py migrated session-state.json (one shared file) to
# one file per session under this directory -- same live-runtime-noise reasoning.
obsidian-fleet-mirror/session-state.d/
!/_scheduled_tasks_backup/

# 2b) Scheduled-task SPECS (SKILL.md only) — versioned 2026-07-12 for durability.
#     Previously unversioned runtime state (repeated loss→restore incidents).
#     ONLY SKILL.md is tracked; runtime state (_state/ _unsent/ logs .bak) stays ignored.
!/scheduled-tasks/
/scheduled-tasks/*
!/scheduled-tasks/*/
/scheduled-tasks/*/*
!/scheduled-tasks/*/SKILL.md
# (2026-07-12) The former E. Fulton HOLD block was removed once the deal was confirmed
# declassified (confidential-terms.json e_fulton active:false, §7b). All scheduled-task
# SKILL.mds are now versioned; runtime state (_state/ _unsent/ logs/.bak) stays ignored below.

# (2026-07-24, Gabriel-authorized) 2b's ONLY-SKILL.md rule caught two non-runtime-state
# files by pattern accident: `ora-task-integrity-guard`'s `guard.ps1`/`membership-guard.ps1`
# engines (found with ZERO backup anywhere, same loss class 2b exists to prevent — fixed via
# force-add, not this rule, since they live one level up from a normal task's structure) and
# these two, found in the same audit: `ora-appsec-sweep/suppressions.md` (Gabriel-ratified
# suppression list — "only Gabriel adds entries here") and `ora-pm-ledger`'s 294-line engine
# script. Per-file allow-list, same shape as the `_shared/` ladder below — NOT a widened
# directory or extension pattern (a `*.ps1`/`*.py` negation here would also sweep in ~40
# deliberately-scratch `_tmp_*`/dated files that live inside individual task folders in
# other contexts and are meant to stay untracked). Name each durable file individually.
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-appsec-sweep/suppressions.md
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-pm-ledger/tenant_db_snapshot_diff.py
# (2026-07-24) Same loss class, same per-file rule: ora-pm-ledger's Block B sweep-window
# planner. Durable engine, not scratch — Block B's coverage tracking is unusable without
# it. Its `state/` sibling stays untracked on purpose: that is per-run data that churns
# daily, and losing it degrades safely to the old today-only window rather than breaking.
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-pm-ledger/outbox_qc_window.py
# (2026-07-27) Same loss class, same per-file rule: ora-pm-ledger's CapEx & Maintenance Log
# snapshot-diff engine — generalizes tenant_db_snapshot_diff.py's approach to a second
# uniform-stamp Notion source (see SKILL.md A.1). Durable engine, not scratch. Its `state/`
# sibling stays untracked on purpose, same reasoning as the two entries above.
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-pm-ledger/capex_snapshot_diff.py
# (2026-08-14) Same loss class, same per-file rule: ora-pm-ledger's Property Register
# snapshot-diff engine — the third and last A.1 uniform-stamp detector (SKILL.md A.2 step 2,
# built 2026-08-11). Durable engine, not scratch: without it a Property Register edit is
# undetectable, because all 14 rows share one batch-write `last_edited_time`. Caught by
# task-folder-backup-completeness-probe (guard STEP 2.8) — it shipped three days after its
# two siblings were force-tracked here and inherited none of their coverage. Its `state/`
# sibling stays untracked on purpose, same reasoning as the three entries above.
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-pm-ledger/property_register_snapshot_diff.py
# (2026-09-17, Gabriel-authorized) Same loss class, arriving by a NEW route: the 2026-09-06
# size-reduction pass moved 13 dated build-history NOTE sections (30KB) out of
# ora-pm-ledger/SKILL.md into references/CHANGELOG-2026-07-08-to-2026-08-16.md, which 2b's
# `/scheduled-tasks/*/*` rule ignores -- so the SKILL.md that cites it is tracked while the
# extracted text it points at sits in no backup at all. Found by dirty-tree-triage while
# committing that SKILL.md. This is a DIRECTORY negation, not the per-file form used above:
# a `!` on a file cannot reach inside a directory the parent rule already excluded. Scope is
# still one task's one folder, and `references/` is durable extracted spec text by
# construction -- the churning per-run data lives in `state/`, which stays ignored.
!/scheduled-tasks/ora-pm-ledger/references/

# (2026-07-17, Gabriel-authorized) 2b covered live task SKILL.mds but not retired ones: a
# _archive_*_retired/<id>/SKILL.md sits one level deeper than */SKILL.md matches, so EVERY
# retired task spec has been silently untracked since 2b shipped -- the identical failure
# mode 2b/2c exist to prevent, just one directory layer down. Same durability rationale,
# same ladder shape, one level deeper, scoped to _archive_* only (does not widen anything
# else under scheduled-tasks/, e.g. _shared/ stays exactly as untracked as before).
!/scheduled-tasks/_archive_*/*/
/scheduled-tasks/_archive_*/*/*
!/scheduled-tasks/_archive_*/*/SKILL.md

# (2026-07-24, Gabriel-authorized) 2b/2c versioned task SPECS and memory, but left
# `_shared/` untracked -- the note above even says so explicitly. That leaves
# `send-via-outlook.ps1`, the SOLE automated send path for BOTH fleets, with no history
# and no backup: every scheduled task's email leg depends on one unversioned file, and an
# accidental truncation would be unrecoverable and silent. Same durability rationale as
# 2b/2c, third occurrence of the same failure mode.
#
# ALLOW-LIST, NOT A DIRECTORY OPT-IN -- deliberately. `_shared/` is a working drawer that
# also holds LIVE CREDENTIALS (`anthropic-key.dat`, `netlify-token.dat`), the full
# `send-log.txt` (every recipient + subject ORA has ever auto-sent), and ~30 `_tmp_*` /
# `hg-*` / dated `.html` email-body scratch files. This repo pushes to GitHub
# (ZeOoba/ora-claude-config), so a directory-level `!` here would publish secrets. Verified
# 2026-07-24: no `*.dat` has ever been committed on any ref. Name each durable file
# individually; never negate the directory, and never add a glob that could catch `*.dat`.
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/send-via-outlook.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/Send-OraReport.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/Send-OutreachQueueEmail.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/EMAIL_DELIVERY.md
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/README-SEND-PATTERN.md
# (2026-08-09) Double-fire entry guard. Same durability rationale as send-via-outlook.ps1:
# these are now load-bearing for ora-am-brief and ora-deal-aging-sla, and an unversioned
# guard that silently truncates fails OPEN -- the tasks would resume double-sending with
# no error anywhere. Named individually per the allow-list rule above; still no glob, and
# nothing here can catch `*.dat`.
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/block-sentinel.py
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/test-block-sentinel.py
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/outlook-sent-probe.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/README-DOUBLE-FIRE-GUARD.md
# (2026-10-18) R-05 send-contract suites. Same per-file rule as above, no glob.
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/send-contract-harness.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/test-send-contract-legacy.ps1
!/scheduled-tasks/_shared/test-send-contract-keyed.ps1

# 2c) Memory (projects/.../memory/) — versioned 2026-07-15 for durability, same rationale
#     as 2b: this /* default-deny (introduced in the SAME 2026-07-12 commit as 2b) never
#     got a matching opt-in for memory, so every memory file except rules.md (already
#     tracked since 2026-07-09, predating the lockdown) silently fell out of git backup —
#     82 files, unnoticed for 3 days, the identical failure mode 2b exists to prevent.
#     Pre-commit checked: 0 active confidential-deal terms found in any memory file
#     (doctrine-integrity-probe.py's own term-matching functions, reused directly; matcher
#     self-check against a synthetic control term confirmed the scan itself works).
#     `projects/` also holds hundreds of raw session-transcript .jsonl files alongside
#     memory/ -- those must NEVER be tracked (arbitrary conversation content). The nested
#     opt-in below reaches memory/ only; everything else under projects/ stays ignored.
!/projects/
/projects/*
!/projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/
/projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/*
!/projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/

# 3) Re-ignore secrets, caches & transient state INSIDE opted-in dirs
hooks/confidential-terms.json
hooks/sessions/
# Cross-session claim broadcast markers (task_17998414, 2026-07-25) -- ephemeral,
# TTL-expired, agent-declared state naming an in-progress subsystem claim. Same
# reasoning as hooks/sessions/ above: this is per-machine runtime state, not
# durable config, and MUST never be re-minted with a stale mtime/timestamp by a
# clone/checkout the way the override sentinels were (668f9d4).
hooks/active-work/
# git-prepush-hook-tests-gate.py's override-frequency counter (OI-136 candidate fix 2,
# 2026-08-12): an append-only JSONL of every allow-hook-test-failure USE, grown on every
# override across the fleet. Same reasoning as maintenance/_state/phantom-dispatch.json
# above (per-machine, ever-growing, read only to compute a rolling-window count) --
# tracking it would churn a commit on every override use for no reader.
hooks/_state/override-usage-log.jsonl
# Per-guard dispatcher telemetry (hooks/lib/guard_telemetry.py, 2026-09-30) -- one
# append per tool call from every session, rotated at ~5 MB to .1. Same class as the
# override-usage log above: per-machine, ever-growing, read only by
# hooks/guard-telemetry-summary.py. Tracking it would churn a commit on every call.
hooks/_state/guard-telemetry.jsonl
hooks/_state/guard-telemetry.jsonl.1
hooks/_state/guard-telemetry.jsonl.rotate.lock
# Human-authorization override sentinels (allow-external-send / allow-website-change /
# allow-doctrine-drift). NEVER track one: the gates validate a sentinel by mtime age, and
# git stamps a checked-out file with CHECKOUT time -- so a tracked token is re-minted with
# age ~0 by every clone/checkout/pull, self-granting a live 60-min override with no human
# in the loop. Not theoretical: 668f9d4 swept a live allow-website-change into the repo and
# a fresh clone was empirically shown to let `netlify deploy --prod` through ora-autonomy-gate
# (exit 0; empty-dir control exit 2) -- an invariant-2 bypass on the MIGRATION_RUNBOOK
# restore path. Guarded by hooks/tests/test-override-token-hygiene.py (2026-07-16).
hooks/overrides/
# generated probe-state snapshot (doctrine-integrity-probe.py / ora-doctrine-integrity-guard);
# rewritten on every run -> was showing as a tracked modification on every single probe fire,
# recurring dirty-tree noise with no review value (2026-07-12 systematization follow-up)
maintenance/_state/doctrine-status.json
# roster-staleness runtime breadcrumb written by confidentiality-guard.py (transient; never track)
hooks/state/
# append-event-log.py's in-place-fallback safety-net copy (overwritten every use;
# recoverable from event_log.md's own git history anyway -- never track, 2026-07-16)
projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/event_log.md.inplace-backup
# append-event-log.py's atomic-insert scratch file. The script now cleans up both of
# these itself; these lines are the backstop for the one case it can't cover -- a kill
# between the tmp write and os.replace. Added 2026-07-16 after a stranded 480KB copy
# showed up as an untracked `??` next to the log, one `git add -A` from being committed.
# (2026-09-12) `.tmp-append.*` added: PR #172 made the name unique per attempt
# (`event_log.md.tmp-append.<pid>.<ns>`), which the exact-name line no longer matched.
# Exact name kept for any pre-#172 copy; anchored to this one basename.
projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/event_log.md.tmp-append
projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/event_log.md.tmp-append.*
# append-event-log.py's lock-contention spool (drains automatically into event_log.md on
# the next successful append; transient by design, never track) -- see 2c above.
projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/event_log.md.pending/
# append-event-log.py's msvcrt byte-range lock sidecar (PR #172, 2026-09-07). Permanent by
# design -- the script never deletes it, only unlocks it -- and 0 bytes: the lock lives on the
# open handle, not in the file. Missed by #172; memory-commit-sweep.py committed it (9ce0923)
# because nothing ignored it. Guarded by hooks/tests/test-memory-sidecar-hygiene.py, which
# derives every sidecar name from the script's source (2026-09-12).
projects/C--Users-Owner-OneDrive---Oxford-Capital-Partners/memory/event_log.md.lock
# county-parcel-screen code is TRACKED (counterparty names externalized 2026-06-11);
# its data/ tree (public county dumps + the pipeline-guard.local.json counterparty
# floor) stays local-only. *.local.json is the standing convention for
# never-track local data files anywhere in the repo.
skills/county-parcel-screen/data/
*.local.json
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.log
*.bak
*.bak-*
# Per-run probe snapshot: rewritten every fleet-deadman cycle, read only on this
# machine. Tracking it would churn a commit a day for no reader (same trap as the
# portfolio.json generatedAt churn). The durable ledger next to it,
# health-guard-beacon.json, IS tracked on purpose -- the GitHub dead-man reads it.
maintenance/_state/phantom-dispatch.json
# Failure marker written by publish-fleet-heartbeat.py when the GitHub publish fails and cleared on
# the next success (OI-323). Read only by fleet-deadman.ps1 on this machine; tracking it would
# commit an outage marker.
maintenance/_state/heartbeat-publish-failed.json
maintenance/_state/heartbeat-publish-failed.json.tmp
maintenance/_state/heartbeat-publish-ok.json
maintenance/_state/heartbeat-publish-ok.json.tmp
# Same pattern, one level down: failure marker / success stamp for push-fleet-heartbeat.ps1's
# backup-push step (OI-331, 2026-09-22). fleet-contract-check.py freshness-checks the ok
# stamp and state-freshness-probe.py holds it to 48h (the failed marker is not-periodic there). Tracking either would commit a
# failure marker or a daily-changing timestamp.
maintenance/_state/backup-push-failed.json
maintenance/_state/backup-push-ok.json
# ora-setup-audit follow-up (2026-08-16, README diagram-review scope): 7 more
# maintenance/_state/ files verified same-class as phantom-dispatch.json above --
# each is regenerated by a LOCAL probe script only (registry-membership-probe.py,
# model-canary-probe.py, model-currency-probe.py, wake-cause-probe.ps1,
